package com.konye.historiq.Core.Localization

/** English-only by design — never translated, per project convention. */
object StringsPrivacyPolicy {
    const val privacy_effective_date: String = "Effective September 28, 2026"
    const val privacy_lede: String = "This page explains, in full, what Historiq collects, why, where it's stored, who it's shared with, and what control you have over it."
    const val privacy_section_overview_title: String = "1. Overview"
    const val privacy_overview_body: String = "Historiq is a daily history-trivia app with six quiz modes, a friends list, per-game stats, and ads shown after a finished game. What data exists about you depends on how you use the app:"
    const val privacy_card_signed_in_label: String = "Signed in with Google, Apple, or Facebook"
    const val privacy_card_signed_in_body: String = "your real name, email, and (for Google or Facebook) profile picture are read once at sign-in and stored, alongside your gameplay and friends data, in a hosted database — so your stats and friend leaderboards work the same across every device you sign into. Apple sign-in is only offered on iPhone/iPad."
    const val privacy_card_guest_label: String = "Continue without login (guest)"
    const val privacy_card_guest_body: String = "no name, email, or other identifying detail is ever read or stored. Your device still gets an anonymous session so your scores and daily progress work correctly — see the Guest mode section below for exactly what that does and doesn't include."
    const val privacy_overview_footer: String = "Everything below states plainly which of these apply to each kind of data."
    const val privacy_section_account_title: String = "2. Account information"
    const val privacy_account_intro: String = "If you sign in with Google, Apple, or Facebook, the app creates an account with:"
    const val privacy_account_item_name: String = "Display name — shown on your profile and to friends on leaderboards"
    const val privacy_account_item_email: String = "Email — your real address from whichever provider you signed in with (or a private relay address, if you chose Apple's \"Hide My Email\" option)"
    const val privacy_account_item_avatar: String = "Profile picture — your real account photo from Google or Facebook, when available; Apple sign-in never provides one"
    const val privacy_account_item_provider: String = "Sign-in provider — which button you tapped (\"Google\", \"Apple\", or \"Facebook\"), display-only"
    const val privacy_account_item_code: String = "Friend code — a random 9-digit code other players can use to add you as a friend"
    const val privacy_account_item_id: String = "A unique account identifier tied to your device's session, so the database can tell your data apart from every other player's"
    const val privacy_account_google_label: String = "About signing in"
    const val privacy_account_google_body: String = "Signing in opens the provider's own account screen — Historiq never sees your Google, Apple, or Facebook password. Each provider returns a signed confirmation of your identity and (if you've shared them with that provider) your name, email, and profile picture — that's the only data Historiq ever receives. Signing in links this to your existing anonymous device session rather than replacing it, so any guest-mode progress from earlier the same day carries over instead of being lost."
    const val privacy_account_mock_label: String = "Platform availability"
    const val privacy_account_mock_body: String = "Google and Facebook sign-in are available on both Android and iPhone/iPad. Apple sign-in is only offered on iPhone/iPad, per Apple's own platform conventions."
    const val privacy_section_gameplay_title: String = "3. Gameplay data"
    const val privacy_gameplay_intro: String = "For every device — guest or signed in — the app stores:"
    const val privacy_gameplay_item_progress: String = "Daily quiz progress for each of the six games — so you can close the app mid-quiz and resume later"
    const val privacy_gameplay_item_score: String = "Your score for each game, for each day you've played it"
    const val privacy_gameplay_footer: String = "This is what makes \"once per game per day\" and same-day resume work reliably, and lets that day's rank (\"28th of 150 players today\") be real rather than simulated — even for a guest, whose entry is tied only to an anonymous device session, never a name or email. Signed-in accounts additionally get lifetime aggregate stats — total games played, average score, and perfect-run count per game — which power the Profile screen and are never collected for guests."
    const val privacy_section_friends_title: String = "4. Friends"
    const val privacy_friends_body: String = "If you send or accept a friend request, the app stores that connection so your Friends leaderboard can show their scores and theirs can show yours. Only accepted friends can see each other's daily scores — no one else can. Friends requires a signed-in account; guest mode has no friend code and no friends list."
    const val privacy_section_advertising_title: String = "5. Advertising"
    const val privacy_advertising_body1: String = "Historiq shows an ad after a finished game, and offers a \"watch an ad for a hint\" option, using Google's AdMob network."
    const val privacy_advertising_body2: String = "To select and measure ads, AdMob (and Google, its ad network) processes your device's advertising ID, general technical information about your device and app, and an approximate location derived from your IP address. Historiq itself never sees or stores this — it flows directly between your device, AdMob, and Google. Ads are not targeted at children and are not shown with child-directed treatment, since Historiq is a general-audience app."
    const val privacy_advertising_body3: String = "You can limit ad personalization for your Google account at any time in your device's system settings (opt out of ads personalization) or at g.co/adssettings."
    const val privacy_section_security_title: String = "6. Security audit log"
    const val privacy_security_body: String = "Every sign-in is logged with its IP address, device/user-agent info, an event label, and a timestamp. This log exists solely to detect abuse and is never readable by the app itself or any client — it's written by a server-side function with no read access granted to anyone."
    const val privacy_section_device_title: String = "7. On-device-only settings"
    const val privacy_device_body: String = "Your language choice, whether haptic vibration is on, whether dark mode is on, and whether the daily reminder is on are stored only in the app's local settings on your device. None of these ever leave your device, whether you're signed in or playing as a guest."
    const val privacy_section_guest_title: String = "8. Guest mode"
    const val privacy_guest_body: String = "Choosing \"Continue without login\" means the app never asks for, reads, or stores any identifying information about you, and never creates a display profile. Your device still gets an anonymous session so scores, daily rank, and the once-a-day play limit work correctly and are shared with no one — but nothing in it is, or can be turned into, your name, email, or any other identifying detail. There's no friend code and no friends list in guest mode, since there's no profile for anyone to connect to. This anonymous session is tied to this device; uninstalling the app or clearing its storage leaves that session unreachable, with no way to sign back into it."
    const val privacy_section_sharing_title: String = "9. Sharing & third parties"
    const val privacy_sharing_body1: String = "Historiq's own database (account, gameplay, and friends data) is never sold, rented, or shared with any third party, advertiser, or analytics service. One processor is used for a specific feature, described in full in the Advertising section above: Google/AdMob, to show ads. It never receives your name or email."
    const val privacy_sharing_body2: String = "If you sign in with Google, Apple, or Facebook, that provider itself is the one exception: it processes your sign-in the same way it does for any app you grant it to, under its own privacy policy, independent of Historiq."
    const val privacy_sharing_body3: String = "The app's \"Share Results\" feature is the other exception worth naming explicitly: tapping it hands an image and a line of text to whatever app you pick from your device's own share sheet — that's a one-time action you trigger yourself, not ongoing data collection."
    const val privacy_section_storage_title: String = "10. Where data is stored"
    const val privacy_storage_body: String = "Accounts, gameplay data, friends, and the security audit log are stored in a hosted Postgres database (via Supabase). Access is restricted by row-level security scoped to your own account and, for scores, your accepted friends — nobody else's client can read your data directly. Guest-mode sessions are stored the same way, but carry no name, email, or other identifying field at all."
    const val privacy_section_choices_title: String = "11. Your choices & deletion"
    const val privacy_choices_item_name: String = "Change your display name anytime from Settings."
    const val privacy_choices_item_toggles: String = "Turn off haptics or the daily reminder anytime from Settings — these take effect immediately."
    const val privacy_choices_item_delete: String = "Delete your account from Settings → Account → Delete Account. This permanently removes your profile, friends, stats, progress, and score history. This cannot be undone."
    const val privacy_choices_item_guest: String = "Guest sessions have no profile to delete through the app, since nothing identifying was ever stored; contact us using the details below if you'd like an anonymous session's gameplay rows removed."
    const val privacy_section_children_title: String = "12. Children's privacy"
    const val privacy_children_body: String = "Historiq is a general-audience trivia app, not directed at children under 13, and isn't listed as a Families app. The app doesn't knowingly collect personal information from children under 13, and ads are never shown with child-directed treatment or targeting. If you believe a child has provided personal information through the app, contact us using the details below and it will be removed."
    const val privacy_section_changes_title: String = "13. Changes to this policy"
    const val privacy_changes_body: String = "If what the app collects or how it's used changes, this screen will be updated and the effective date above will change accordingly."
    const val privacy_section_contact_title: String = "14. Contact"
    const val privacy_contact_body: String = "Questions about this policy, or requests to access or delete your data, can be sent to:"
    const val privacy_view_online_label: String = "This policy is also available online at:"
}
